WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.060
Film Courage: Why do you think it’s so important
for so many filmmakers to make that first

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.140
feature film by age 30?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:13.130
Adam Davis, Writer/Director: That’s a fantastic
question.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:14.840
By 30?

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:20.730
Your 20’s are about sort of figuring out
who you are and a lot of trial and error and

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.390
a lot of messing up.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:26.890
When you hit your 30’s you sort of have
that feeling of like you have to have it all

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:27.890
together right?

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.619
Like you’re an adult now, you’re an official
adult.

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:34.270
You are not in the 20’s like put all of
that childish stuff behind.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.510
You’re in the 30’s.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:40.050
You’re thinking about building a family
and doing all of that and you’re really

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:48.410
thinking about your career and sort of how
you advance in that whatever that may be (day

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.350
job or filmmaking).

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:56.410
So I think it’s that pressure of turning
30 and feeling like you need to become a full

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:02.140
adult that pushes people to go well I need
to do a feature now.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.740
And I think some people may be ready for that
just as developmentally some people are still

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:13.119
going to be toying around and doing all this
club life and dating and stuff in their 30’s

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:17.900
which you know you can do or should do or
have to do depending on your situation.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.600
Film Courage: Or in the 60’s.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.970
They could be in their 60’s doing that.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:22.970
Adam: Exactly.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.130
It really just depends on the person.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:29.340
Film Courage: Yes, I think a lot of it…well…it
seems like comes from family pressure or that

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.580
traditional sort of idea and people don’t
want to let their family unit down and some

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:41.240
people don’t have that and maybe they wish
they had it because they are so carefree.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.219
So I think there are two sides of the coin
in that.

00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:47.370
I’ve seen a lot of people who come from
a very close-knit families where they really

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:51.860
want their kids to succeed but then there
is that pressure where you’ve got to grow

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.280
up by age 30.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.039
So I think when you’re creative it might
be tricky because you want to please the people

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:04.079
that you love but at the same time you’re
still an artist in some way.

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:05.320
So you’ve managed to do both.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:10.520
You’ve done a lot of short films, you’ve
made a feature film and you’ve said that

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.890
you were PAing [Production Assistant] sometimes
and were working 15 hour days?

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.410
Adam: Yes, back when I first moved out to
Los Angeles.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.160
I didn’t go to film school.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.630
I went to a Journalism school in Des Moines,
Iowa where I was part of the Radio and TV

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.480
production program so I learned a lot technically.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:39.330
But what I didn’t learn was how to make
films or how to tell stories in that regard.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.580
What I knew that I needed once I moved out
to Los Angeles was real on-set experience.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.220
How is a movie set run?

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:50.350
What makes a good movie set or makes a bad
movie set in terms of operating because if

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:55.270
I am going to be running this one day I need
to know what styles of work I like?

00:02:55.270 --> 00:03:00.450
What styles of work I don’t and when you
start at the very bottom and you sort of see

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:06.740
everything from that low position and you
see how people are treated and you have those

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:15.760
eyes into everything I think it’s a really
good look at how I want my sets to run.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:23.640
The one set that I worked on which really
set a high example of what an amazing film

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:31.140
set is was when I worked on A SERIOUS MAN
(The Coen Brothers movie).

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.920
It was filming back in Minnesota and I was
like this is an amazing opportunity.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.050
I’ve got to get on this set, right?

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:44.560
So I found a way to connect to the ADs and
I told them I know you may need PA’s from

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.560
out there.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:46.780
I’m from there, I’m from Minnesota.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:50.010
I will move back there while it’s being
shot.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.010
You don’t have to put me up.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.010
You don’t have to do anything.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.010
I will move back there while it’s being
shot.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.460
Just let me work on a Coen Brothers’ set.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:56.660
And they were like cool.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.080
Come on board!

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.080
I did it.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:07.980
I spent a month out there and just being able
to see how the Coen’s worked and just seeing

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:16.960
Roger Deakins work for one was just amazing
but being able to see how respectful everybody

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.120
was, how nobody yelled, how quiet the set
was.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.650
How everybody did their job.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:30.140
It was a very familial feeling set.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.690
It was just eye-opening.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:38.280
Because you’ve been…at least I have been
on those sets of low-budget things and Hallmark

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:45.340
movies and big movies and all in-between TV
shows and you see the tensions and the stress

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:49.710
and the yelling and the screaming and the
disrespect.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.680
The Coens’ on A SERIOUS MAN they had none
of that.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.280
It was the smoothest running set.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.740
Everybody was really on board.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.350
It was a pleasure to be there.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:01.930
Film Courage: How cool.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:09.000
Sorry, you were in LA and you thought let
me go back…please let me be on the set and

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.000
you went back home?

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.680
Adam: One hundred percent.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.880
There was no other way I was going to get
on that set.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:15.970
I had to sacrifice.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.600
I was like I will fly myself out there and
stay at my parent’s house.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.570
It will be fun to see everybody again but
just let me work on this movie please.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.360
Film Courage: Did you stay at your parent’s
house?

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.360
Adam: I did, yes.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.750
Film Courage: What time would you leave to
go to set?

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:38.040
Adam: Oh man, I would leave around 5:00 a.m.
to get to set around 5:30 a.m. or 6:00 a.m.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:43.140
because the PA’s [Production Assistant]
is like the first on set, last to leave.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.110
So just sort of driving through the night
and getting to whatever location whether it

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:52.770
was the suburban street that the family lived
on or the park where they are down by the

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:59.900
lake it was fun and it allowed me to see a
different part of Minnesota and the place

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.260
that I grew up than I would have otherwise.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:08.400
You realize that there is this whole strip
of a neighborhood that has houses that were

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.830
just still look like they are from that era
(the 60’s and 70’s).

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:21.180
It’s really trippy and opens your eyes about
what is around, what is available in your

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.180
hometown.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:27.280
Film Courage: And how was it to see the actors
between takes because that like I think the

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.800
first set I was on was Melrose Place…well
aren’t there two of them?

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.550
The nineties one.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:39.050
For me seeing what it was actually like, turning
the camera, okay let’s try the shot from

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:40.050
another angle?

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.080
How was this for you?

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:47.270
Adam: It was cool when I could see it because
as a PA sometimes you don’t necessarily

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.710
get to be that close to set.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:55.620
Sometimes you are stuck way out you know blocking
traffic or making sure that people don’t

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.020
invade the set or whatever.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:04.210
But when I got to sort of see some of the
smaller scenes with the actors it was really

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:11.710
cool to see how excited everybody was to be
there and how the Coen’s worked with everybody

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:15.400
and how easy it sort of seemed.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:21.540
That’s what was interesting because for
me working with actors (especially established

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:29.030
actors) as somebody who hasn’t done that
many films it’s a little freaky.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:32.430
You’re like you’ve done a lot.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:33.900
What have I done?

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:41.760
It’s a little intimidating but they made
it seem so easy which is what I sort of want

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.760
to strive for.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.639
Film Courage: So when you wrapped how was
that?

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:47.750
You had to fly back to LA?

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.190
Adam: Yes, yes.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:55.680
So I spent about a month at home and then
my time was done, back to LA to try to find

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.050
the next job you know?

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:01.740
Because that is what it is when you’re a
PA it’s like making those connections on

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:06.490
set, trying to get work and pinging those
connections when you are out of work and trying

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:13.660
to find out what to do so it’s a really
terrifying thing that I’m glad I did when

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:17.090
I was young and I sort of did not know any
better and I was just full of this head of

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:21.490
steam of like Alright I am going to make this
happen no matter what and you just sort of

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:22.870
because you are fearless in that regard.

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:27.440
Even though you are full of fear which is
an interesting dichotomy like you’re just

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:34.070
going for it because you have to survive,
like survival instincts are in and are very

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:38.750
active because you’ve got to make rent,
you’ve got to be able to eat.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:44.420
So getting that work is sort of a life or
death type of thing.

